":"CAR","distance":0.378,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2019,"team":"WAS","distance":0.3965,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"DAL","distance":0.417,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.42,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Brian Daboll"}],"defense":[{"season":2018,"team":"NO","distance":0.8134,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2021,"team":"IND","distance":0.8593,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"TEN","distance":0.8714,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennard Wilson"},{"season":2022,"team":"JAX","distance":0.8748,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"SEA","distance":0.8775,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Mike Macdonald"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jets went 2-14 in 2020 and were outscored 457 to 243. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 32nd in EPA per play and the defense 25th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive trait was the 2nd highest nickel usage in the league.","offense":"The Jets offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Sam Darnold ran the passing game, with Jamison Crowder leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Jets defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 10th and 29th. Jordan Jenkins was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Tarell Basham, who had 3.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest nickel usage in the league.","special":"The Jets were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 75% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Sam Darnold played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,208 yards. Targets were spread around, with Jamison Crowder and Breshad Perriman leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Frank Gore and La'Mical Perine. On defense, Quinnen Williams led the team with 7 sacks and Pierre Desir picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Buccaneers under Josh Grizzard and the 2025 Panthers under Dave Canales. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Saints under Dennis Allen and the 2021 Colts.","trends":"The offense went from 31st in EPA per play in 2019 to 32nd in 2020, about where it was. The defense moved from 10th in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 25th in 2020, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was pistol usage, which went from 2% to 0%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}